==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
First attested in the mid 15th century. From {{inh|en|enm|accusative}}, from {{der|en|xno|accusatif}} or {{der|en|frm|acusatif}} or from {{der|en|la|accūsātīvus|t=having been blamed}}, from {{m|la|accūsō|t=to blame}}.  Equivalent to {{suffix|en|accuse|ative}}. The Latin form is a mistranslation of the {{der|en|grc|-}} grammatical term {{m|grc|αἰτιᾱτική|t=expressing an effect}}. This term actually comes from {{affix|grc|αἰτιᾱτός|t1=caused|-ῐκός|pos2=adjective suffix|nocat=1}}, but was reanalyzed as coming from {{m|grc||αἰτιᾱ-}}, the stem of the verb {{m|grc|αἰτιάομαι|t=to blame}}, + {{m|grc|-τῐκός|pos=verbal adjective suffix}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkjuːzətɪv/|a=UK}}
* {{enPR|əkū'zətĭv|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/əˈkjuzətɪv/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ac|cusa|tive}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-accusative.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Producing [[accusation]]s; in a manner that reflects a finding of fault or blame
#: {{syn|en|accusatory|accusatorial}}
#* {{quote-text|en|date=22 November 1641|author=w:Edward Dering|title=a speech|passage=This hath been a very '''accusative''' age.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=14 April 84|journal=Gay Community News|page=4|author=William F. Orrell|title=Bad Business|text=The proprietor of the store was rude, insulting and '''accusative'''.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar}} Applied to the [[case]] (as the fourth case of Latin, Lithuanian and Greek nouns) which expresses the [[immediate]] [[direct object|object]] on which the action or influence of a [[transitive]] verb has its limited influence. Other parts of speech, including secondary or [[predicate]] [[direct object]]s, will also influence a sentence’s construction. In German the case used for [[direct object]]s.

